Secure Boot provides a way to prevent unnoticed tampering with the bootchain.
Using Secure Boot requires signing different parts of the bootchain and
verifying their signature before execution.

Device firmware can also be factory-signed and make use of Secure Boot for
verification. In these cases, Microsoft's keys have been used for signing and
not having their certificates enrolled, will result in the device not working.
As a worst case, this could cause your entire system to be bricked. For this
reason, we will also enroll Microsoft's keys. This is non-optional as it has no
downsides and could prevent dramatic damage to your system.

For the reason listed above, Secure Boot can be dangerous if set up improperly
and on devices with factory-signed firmware. This script takes the necessary
precautions to bypass any potentially troublesome effects of setting up Secure
Boot. However, you yourself are responsible for making sure you know what you
are doing when running this script. I take no responsibility for any damage
your system may sustain.

